// std/target — the two axes a Vader build is parameterised by.
//
// This module holds ONLY the vocabulary, because two very different things need
// to speak it and they live in different layers:
//
//   - `VADER_OS` / `VADER_ARCH` — the COMPILATION target, baked at build time,
//     and what `@target` filters on.
//   - `current_os()` — the OS the program is actually RUNNING on, answered at
//     runtime by the C runtime.
//
// They are not the same question: under `--target=windows-x86_64` from a Mac the
// two differ, and a program that manipulates its own environment — resolving a
// path, spawning a process — wants the second. But they must share a type, or
// `current_os() == VADER_OS` could not be written, and that comparison is
// exactly what a cross-compiling toolchain asks. Sharing a type is why the
// enums live here in `std/` rather than a layer up: `std/` may not import
// `toolchain/`, and `current_os()` belongs to `std/`.
//
// What is NOT here: `Target`, the pair, and the `<os>-<arch>` parsing the CLI
// does. Those are compilation vocabulary — a program computing numbers has no
// reason to parse `"darwin-arm64"` — and they live in `vader/target` until a
// build driver asks for them.

module "std/target"

/// An operating system a Vader program can be built for, or run on.
///
/// `wasm` is deliberately absent: it is an ARCHITECTURE, and the two systems
/// that run on it are far enough apart that one value would hide the difference
/// that matters — `Wasi` has a POSIX-shaped filesystem, `Browser` has none at
/// all. Code that selects on the OS needs to tell them apart.
export Os :: enum(u8) {
    Windows,
    Linux,
    Darwin,
    Wasi,
    Browser,
}

/// An instruction set.
///
/// 32-bit native targets are absent on purpose. The divergences they carry —
/// stdcall name decoration, WoW64 filesystem redirection — are specific to
/// 32-bit Windows, and they are the only cases where the architecture would
/// have had to select CODE rather than parameterise values. Dropping them is
/// what lets `@target` filter on the OS alone.
export Arch :: enum(u8) {
    X86_64,
    Arm64,
    Wasm32,
}

// Canonical spelling, and the one the `<os>-<arch>` command-line form uses.
// Drives `"${os}"` interpolation, `println(os)`, and the parsing in
// `vader/target`, which round-trips against it.
Os implements Display -> match self {
    .Windows -> "windows"
    .Linux   -> "linux"
    .Darwin  -> "darwin"
    .Wasi    -> "wasi"
    .Browser -> "browser"
}

Arch implements Display -> match self {
    .X86_64 -> "x86_64"
    .Arm64  -> "arm64"
    .Wasm32 -> "wasm32"
}

// ---- the running platform -------------------------------------------------

/// The operating system this process is RUNNING on.
///
/// Not the compilation target. `VADER_OS` answers that one, is baked at build
/// time, and is what `@target` selects on; the two differ whenever a build is
/// cross-compiled, and they differ for the compiler itself every time it emits
/// for a platform other than its own.
///
/// Use this one whenever the answer is about the environment the program finds
/// itself in — resolving a path, choosing a separator, deciding whether a binary
/// carries `.exe`. Using `VADER_OS` there is a bug that only shows up on a
/// cross-compiled build, which is the hardest kind to notice.
///
/// The runtime returns the variant's ordinal, so this enum's declaration order
/// is part of the contract with `runtime/c/vader.h::vader_current_os` — the same
/// coupling `std/io::Stream` already has with the write primitive.
///
/// @return  the host OS, never null: an unsupported platform fails at C compile
///          time rather than guessing.
@intrinsic export current_os :: fn() -> Os

// ---- tests ----------------------------------------------------------------

import "std/testing"

// ⚠️ These call `to_string()` rather than interpolating `"${Os.Darwin}"`, which
// yields the ORDINAL instead of the Display form — a compiler bug, recorded in
// TODO. Interpolating a variable works; only the qualified literal is affected.
@test test_os_display_is_the_command_line_spelling :: fn() -> void {
    assert_eq(Os.Darwin.to_string(), "darwin")
    assert_eq(Os.Browser.to_string(), "browser")
}

@test test_arch_display_keeps_the_underscore :: fn() -> void {
    // `x86_64`, not `x86-64`: the `-` is the axis separator in `<os>-<arch>`,
    // so an arch name may not contain one.
    assert_eq(Arch.X86_64.to_string(), "x86_64")
}
